        /**
         * Ported from api.rs's `reset_state` (`:2733-2766`) plus the teardown half of
         * `rustpush_service.dart`'s `reset`/`disposeState` (`:5211-5290`).
         *
         * BB takes the poll canceller, config, APS connection and account as four separate parameters
         * because Dart holds all four itself; here they all live inside the session, so it takes the
         * session. The FRB `dispose()` calls that make up most of `disposeState` have no equivalent --
         * those free Dart-side handles to Rust objects, which is exactly what this port's single opaque
         * handle avoids by construction. What does carry over is the pair of real closes at the end:
         * `close_client` and `close_aps`, which are what actually stop the event loop and drop the push
         * connection (there is no separate cancel channel here -- see `eventloop.rs`'s `recv_wait`,
         * which ends on `LoopSignal::Closed`).
         *
         * [logout] additionally deregisters this device from iMessage and signs the Apple account out
         * everywhere, and [reset_hw] throws away the activated hardware so setup starts again from
         * device activation rather than resuming the saved device. Either one deletes the saved account:
         * a logout because that is what it is, a hardware reset because the account's continuation key
         * belongs to the device being thrown away. A hardware reset without a logout sets the account
         * aside first, for the device that replaces this one to sign in as; a logout removes that too.
